# Great cormorant

The great cormorant is a large, dark seabird of coasts, estuaries and inland
waters across Europe, Asia, Africa and eastern North America. It feeds by
descending from the surface and propelling itself through the water after eels,
flatfish and sprats, steering with the tail and driving with fully webbed feet.
A descent lasts twenty to forty seconds, and the prey is brought back up and
manipulated head-first before being swallowed. The wettable plumage that makes
the descent efficient must be dried afterwards, which is why the species stands
with its wings held open. See [[phalacrocoracidae]].
